(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Bragg's Law of X-ray diffraction. Derive the mathematical condition for constructive interference of X-rays scattered from parallel crystal planes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Background:) Tj
T*
(   In 1913, W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg treated X-ray diffraction as the specular reflection of) Tj
T*
(   monochromatic X-rays from successive parallel atomic lattice planes.) Tj
T*
(   Because X-ray wavelengths \(lambda ~ 0.1 nm\) are of the same order as atomic interplanar) Tj
T*
(   spacings \(d ~ 0.2 nm\), constructive interference occurs at specific angles.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Geometric Derivation of Bragg's Law:) Tj
T*
(   Consider two parallel crystal lattice planes P1 and P2 separated by perpendicular interplanar) Tj
T*
(   distance d.) Tj
T*
(   Let a parallel monochromatic X-ray beam of wavelength lambda strike plane P1 at glancing angle) Tj
T*
(   theta \(measured from the plane surface\).) Tj
T*
(   - Ray 1 strikes atom A on plane P1 and reflects at angle theta.) Tj
T*
(   - Ray 2 penetrates and strikes atom B on plane P2 and reflects at angle theta.) Tj
T*
(   To find the path difference Delta between Ray 1 and Ray 2, drop perpendiculars from A onto Ray) Tj
T*
(   2:) Tj
T*
(   Perpendicular AM onto incident ray 2, and AN onto reflected ray 2.) Tj
T*
(   Path difference: Delta = MB + BN.) Tj
T*
(   In right triangle AMB: angle MAB = theta, AB = d => MB = d * sin\(theta\).) Tj
T*
(   In right triangle ANB: angle NAB = theta, AB = d => BN = d * sin\(theta\).) Tj
T*
(   Total Path Difference: Delta = d * sin\(theta\) + d * sin\(theta\) = 2 * d * sin\(theta\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Constructive Interference Condition:) Tj
T*
(   For the reflected waves to reinforce constructively and produce an intense diffraction peak,) Tj
T*
(   the path difference must equal an integer number of wavelengths:) Tj
T*
(   Delta = n * lambda, where n = 1, 2, 3... is the order of reflection.) Tj
T*
(   Bragg's Law: 2 * d * sin\(theta\) = n * lambda.) Tj
T*
(   Limitations: Since sin\(theta\) <= 1, diffraction can occur only if lambda <= 2*d.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
