(   [Problem Statement]: Outline the Classical Free Electron Theory \(Drude-Lorentz Model\). State its basic postulates, derive the expression for electrical conductivity sigma = n*e^2*tau/m, and explain its major experimental failures.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Fundamental Postulates of Drude-Lorentz Model \(1900\):) Tj
T*
(   - Metals consist of stationary positive ion cores forming a rigid lattice, surrounded by a gas) Tj
T*
(   of valence 'free' conduction electrons.) Tj
T*
(   - Mutual electron-electron repulsions and electron-ion attractions are neglected \(free electron) Tj
T*
(   approximation\).) Tj
T*
(   - Electrons execute random thermal motion with mean speed v_th obeying Maxwell-Boltzmann) Tj
T*
(   classical statistics.) Tj
T*
(   - Collisions with ion cores are instantaneous and elastic; mean time between consecutive) Tj
T*
(   collisions is relaxation time tau.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Derivation of Electrical Conductivity \(sigma = n*e^2*tau / m\):) Tj
T*
(   Apply an external electric field E along x-axis. Each electron experiences electrostatic force:) Tj
T*
(   F = - e * E.) Tj
T*
(   By Newton's Second Law: m * \(dv_d / dt\) = - e * E => acceleration a = - e * E / m.) Tj
T*
(   Over average collision time tau, electrons acquire average directional drift velocity v_d:) Tj
T*
(   v_d = a * tau = - \( e * E * tau \) / m.) Tj
T*
(   Electric current density J is defined as total charge crossing unit area per unit second:) Tj
T*
(   J = - n * e * v_d = - n * e * [ - \(e * E * tau\) / m ] = \( n * e^2 * tau / m \) * E.) Tj
T*
(   Comparing with Ohm's microscopic law J = sigma * E:) Tj
T*
(   sigma = \( n * e^2 * tau \) / m.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Failures of Classical Drude Theory:) Tj
T*
(   - Electronic Specific Heat Catastrophe: Classical equipartition theorem predicts electrons) Tj
T*
(   contribute 3/2 R to molar heat capacity. Experimentally, electronic specific heat is ~100 times) Tj
T*
(   smaller \(~0.01 R\) at room temperature.) Tj
T*
(   - Paramagnetic Susceptibility: Classical theory predicts Curie law susceptibility chi) Tj
T*
(   proportional to 1/T. Experimentally, Pauli spin paramagnetism is temperature-independent.) Tj
T*
(   - Wiedemann-Franz Law breakdown at low temperatures: The experimental Lorenz number L deviates) Tj
T*
(   sharply from classical value at cryo-temperatures.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
