(   [Problem Statement]: A superconducting solenoid magnet constructed of Nb-Ti wire \(T_c = 9.2 K, H_c\(0\) = 1.2 * 10^7 A/m\) operates at liquid helium temperature T = 4.2 K. Calculate: \(1\) The critical magnetic field H_c at 4.2 K, \(2\) If the solenoid has 2500 turns per meter, compute the maximum allowable operating current I_max before quenching.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Critical Magnetic Field at T = 4.2 K \(Tuyn's Formula\):) Tj
T*
(   H_c\(T\) = H_c\(0\) * [ 1 - \( T / T_c \)^2 ].) Tj
T*
(   Given H_c\(0\) = 1.2 * 10^7 A/m, T = 4.2 K, T_c = 9.2 K:) Tj
T*
(   \( T / T_c \)^2 = \( 4.2 / 9.2 \)^2 = \( 0.45652 \)^2 = 0.20841.) Tj
T*
(   H_c\(4.2 K\) = 1.2 * 10^7 * [ 1 - 0.20841 ] = 1.2 * 10^7 * 0.79159 = 9.499 * 10^6 A/m =~ 9.50 *) Tj
T*
(   10^6 A/m.) Tj
T*
(   In terms of magnetic flux density B_c = mu_0 * H_c \(mu_0 = 4*pi*10^-7 H/m\):) Tj
T*
(   B_c = \(4 * pi * 10^-7\) * 9.499 * 10^6 = 11.937 Tesla =~ 11.94 Tesla.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Maximum Allowable Solenoid Operating Current I_max:) Tj
T*
(   The internal axial magnetic field inside an ideal solenoid is given by:) Tj
T*
(   H = n_turns * I, where n_turns = 2500 turns/meter.) Tj
T*
ET

