(   [Problem Statement]: A SQUID magnetometer is used to measure magnetic field variations in structural health monitoring of reinforced concrete bridges. The SQUID sensing loop has an area A = 2.0 mm^2. Calculate: \(1\) The magnetic field change Delta B corresponding to the passage of exactly one magnetic flux quantum Phi_0, \(2\) If the instrument can resolve a phase change of 10^-4 of a flux quantum, calculate the minimum detectable magnetic field.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Magnetic Field Change for One Flux Quantum \(Delta B_single\):) Tj
T*
(   By magnetic flux relation: Phi = B * A => Delta Phi = Delta B * A.) Tj
T*
(   For Delta Phi = Phi_0 = h / \(2*e\) = 2.06783 * 10^-15 Weber \(T*m^2\):) Tj
T*
(   Given sensing loop area A = 2.0 mm^2 = 2.0 * 10^-6 m^2:) Tj
T*
(   Delta B_single = Phi_0 / A = \( 2.06783 * 10^-15 T*m^2 \) / \( 2.0 * 10^-6 m^2 \).) Tj
T*
(   Delta B_single = 1.0339 * 10^-9 Tesla = 1.034 Nanotesla \(nT\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Minimum Detectable Magnetic Field \(B_min\):) Tj
T*
(   The magnetometer resolves fractional flux variations: Delta Phi_min = 10^-4 * Phi_0.) Tj
T*
(   Delta Phi_min = 10^-4 * \(2.06783 * 10^-15\) = 2.06783 * 10^-19 Weber.) Tj
T*
(   B_min = Delta Phi_min / A = \( 2.06783 * 10^-19 \) / \( 2.0 * 10^-6 \).) Tj
T*
(   B_min = 1.0339 * 10^-13 Tesla = 103.4 Femtotesla \(fT\) =~ 0.103 Picotesla \(pT\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Significance:) Tj
T*
(   Earth's ambient geomagnetic field is ~ 50 microtesla \(5 * 10^-5 T\).) Tj
T*
(   A SQUID can detect magnetic perturbations 500 million times weaker than Earth's magnetic field,) Tj
T*
(   enabling non-destructive evaluation of internal rebar corrosion, fatigue microcracks, and) Tj
T*
(   stress-induced magnetic domain reorientations in civil infrastructure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
